Shark records your teams' radio broadcasts with a Griffin RadioSHARK, streams
games live, and serves the recordings as a podcast.

- The schedule service pulls NFL, NHL, NBA and MLB schedules for the teams in
  teams.toml and merges them by priority: a game that overlaps one from a team
  listed above it is skipped whole.
- The recorder tunes the shark and records each scheduled game to MP3, streaming
  it to Icecast while it records.
- The web app serves a dashboard, the podcast feed (/feed.xml), the recordings,
  and the live stream (/live). Every URL it hands out is built from the host the
  request arrived on, so the same server works on a LAN address, behind a
  reverse proxy that sets X-Forwarded-Proto, or over Tailscale.


Running it
----------

Needs Docker with Compose, a RadioSHARK on USB, and a Linux host whose kernel
has the radio-shark (or shark2) and snd-usb-audio drivers. Images are built for
amd64 and arm64 (64-bit Raspberry Pi OS works).

    mkdir shark && cd shark
    curl -LO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DerekParks/shark/master/docker-compose.yaml
    curl -L -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DerekParks/shark/master/sample.env
    mkdir config
    curl -L -o config/teams.toml https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DerekParks/shark/master/teams.example.toml

Edit config/teams.toml (the comments explain each field) and set the passwords
and TZ in .env, then:

    docker compose up -d

Then open http://<host>:8771. Check the teams file with
`docker compose run --rm schedule python buildSchedule.py --check --teams=/config/teams.toml`.

Before trusting it with a game, check reception. AM and FM can differ a lot
depending on where the shark sits:

    docker compose run --rm recorder sh -c 'fm -q 0.85 && arecord -D hw:CARD=RadioSHARK,DEV=0 -d 10 -c 2 -f S16_LE -r 44100 /data/test.wav'

fm takes MHz, so 850 AM is 0.85. Listen to data/test.wav.


Configuration
-------------

config/teams.toml   Teams, stations and priority. See teams.example.toml.
config/cover.jpg    Optional podcast cover; set SHARK_COVER=/config/cover.jpg.
.env                Everything else; sample.env lists every setting:

  ICECAST_*_PASSWORD      Icecast passwords (required)
  COMPOSE_PROFILES        "recorder" to run the recorder (needs the shark)
  TZ                      Time zone for file names and episode dates (UTC)
  SHARK_FEED_TITLE        Feed and dashboard title (Shark Sports)
  SHARK_FEED_DESCRIPTION  Feed description
  SHARK_FEED_AUTHOR       Feed author (Shark)
  SHARK_COVER             Cover image path inside the container
  SHARK_WEB_PORT          Host port for the web app (8771)
  SHARK_ALSA_DEVICE       Capture device (hw:CARD=RadioSHARK,DEV=0)
  SHARK_RADIO_DEVICE      Tuner device on the host (/dev/radio0)
  SHARK_BITRATE           Recording bitrate, kbps (32)
  SHARK_STREAM_BITRATE    Live stream bitrate, kbps (64)
  PUID, PGID              User the containers run as (1000)
  AUDIO_GID, VIDEO_GID    Host groups owning /dev/snd and /dev/radio0 (29, 44)
  ICECAST_PORT            Icecast admin port on 127.0.0.1 (8770)

Recordings land in data/out as <team>-<date>-<HHMM>.mp3. A game the recorder
had to rejoin after a restart continues in <team>-<date>-<HHMM>-2.mp3.


Development
-----------

    uv sync
    uv run pytest

archive/ holds the original command-line RadioSHARK control app. More about it:
http://javier.rodriguez.org.mx/index.php/2006/06/10/griffin-radio-shark-icecast2-on-debian-gnulinux
Its original README states a Public Domain licence.

All other code is licensed under CC0 (https://wiki.creativecommons.org/CC0).
